# =============================================================================
# inf — a literal must fit its slot
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • An integer literal of any length is a constant, and it lands only where
#     its exact value fits. `18446744073709551616` is 2^64: one past `u64`,
#     so it does not fit `numerus` (`numerus_literal_out_of_range`).
#   • The fix is the opt-in `inf` slot: `let inf x ← 18446744073709551616`.
#   • No literal quietly becomes an `inf`; the slot decides.
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • leaving the slot unannotated — an unannotated big literal defaults to
#     `numerus` and is rejected the same way.
#
# See also: inf, numerus, u64
# =============================================================================

# typi/inf-literal-range-error — a literal beyond the slot's range
#
# WHY: pins F9 rulings 34 and 47 at `faber check`: the lexer accepts the
# long literal, the slot rule rejects it by issue name.
#
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
#   inf-literal-range-error.expected — the stable diagnostic identity.

main {
    let int too_big ← 18446744073709551616
    print too_big
}
